      en: "Mount Cameroon, locally known as 'Mongo ma Ndemi' (Mountain of Greatness), is the highest peak in West and Central Africa, standing at 4,040 meters. It is an active stratovolcano that rises steeply from the Gulf of Guinea, showcasing a unique ecological sequence from tropical rainforests to alpine meadows. The volcanic activity is well-documented, with significant eruptions occurring in 1999, 2000, and most recently in 2012, which often sent lava flows near the coastline. The mountain is renowned for its extreme annual rainfall, particularly on its western slopes near Debundscha, making it one of the wettest places on Earth. Culturally, the volcano is deeply rooted in the myths of the Bakweri people, who believe the mountain spirit, Efasa Moto, controls the eruptions. Every year, the famous 'Mount Cameroon Race of Hope' takes place, an extreme marathon to the summit and back. The Mount Cameroon National Park protects its rich biodiversity, including rare orchid species and forest elephants inhabiting the lower elevations.",
